@startuml one-host-per-node
!include ../_includes/theme.puml

title One host per node with per-session holders (proposed)

actor "Operator" as OP $PERSON

node "Node (one OS user)" {
  component "Session host\nsupervisor, registry, doorbell router,\naccess control, event stream" as HOST $SERVICE
  file "Control socket\n0600 in a 0700 directory\npeer credentials checked" as SOCK $PRIVATE

  rectangle "Main loop" as ML {
    component "Holder" as H0 $SERVICE
    component "Main loop session\n(capability: registered root sessions)" as S0 $AGENT
  }
  rectangle "Process A" as PA {
    component "Holder" as H1 $SERVICE
    component "Root session A\n(capability: its own subtree)" as S1 $AGENT
    component "Holder" as H1b $SERVICE
    component "Owner-run session\n(sub-orchestrator)" as S1b $AGENT
    database "Board A" as B1 $COMMITTED
  }
  rectangle "Process B" as PB {
    component "Holder" as H2 $SERVICE
    component "Root session B" as S2 $AGENT
    database "Board B" as B2 $COMMITTED
  }
}

OP --> SOCK : keeper attach, park, stop, estop\n(operator authority)
S0 --> SOCK : wake, park\n(capability token)
SOCK --> HOST
HOST --> H0 : private holder channel\n(secret via inherited fd)
HOST --> H1
HOST --> H1b
HOST --> H2
H0 --> S0 : owns the terminal
H1 --> S1 : owns the terminal
H1b --> S1b : owns the terminal
H2 --> S2 : owns the terminal
S1 --> B1 : writes
S1b --> B1 : writes its leased subtree
S2 --> B2 : writes

note right of HOST
  Restarting or upgrading the host
  leaves every holder running;
  the new host re-adopts them.
  One registry serves attach,
  doorbells, supervision and recovery.
end note

caption Sources: spec §5, §6; today: one daemon per board (spec §2.1, §2.8)
@enduml
